/*  The Cufet type checker, written in Cufet.

        cufet self-hosting/Cufet/checker/checker.cufe [--tree] <file.cufe> [more…]

    Prints one line for each file, after its `file` header:

        ok                      the C# checker accepts it
                                (with `--tree`, followed by the checked program, one statement
                                to a line, printed as `ParserTreePrinter` prints C#'s)
        error   <message>       it refuses it, in these words (a line break written `\n`)
        unsupported <what>      this checker cannot judge it yet
        parse   <message>       the parser refused it, so there was nothing to check

    ★★ `ok` IS A CLAIM, and the one this checker is slowest to make. A refusal is found at one place,
    but acceptance is a statement about the whole program — so any construct not yet judged makes
    the file `unsupported` rather than `ok`, and `CheckerParityTests` counts only real agreement.
*/

Pull a book on collections.
    /// A refusal's words on one line, the way the parity test compares them.
    Bind text to one-line, given (the text raw):
        Define flat as replace "\\" with "\\\\" in raw.
        Return replace "\n" with "\\n" in flat.
    Done.

    /// The one line printed for a program.
    Bind text to verdict, given (the text whole, the series of node prelude, the fact with-tree, the text source-file,
            the map from text to node shared-trees):
        Define parsed as cast listed.
        Define starts as cast listed.
        Try to:
            Define both as cast parser's program-and-starts on (whole).
            The parsed becomes item 1 of both's children.
            The starts becomes item 2 of both's children.
        Done.
        In case of failure:
            Return "parse\t{cast one-line on (the message of the failure)}".
        Done.
        // Style advice is read from the program as written — before checking writes into it — and
        // said only about a program that checks.
        Define lint-tokens as cast tokens-of on (whole) but on failure a series of token.
        Define warnings as cast lint-warnings on (lint-tokens, starts, cast copied-node on (parsed)).
        Define outcome as "ok".
        Try to:
            The outcome becomes cast checked on (parsed, prelude, with-tree, source-file, shared-trees).
        Done.
        In case of failure:
            Define said as the message of the failure.
            The outcome becomes said.
            If said starts with "error\t":
                The outcome becomes "error\t{cast one-line on (the characters from 7 to the end of said)}".
            Done.
        Done.
        // Each warning on a line of its own, right after `ok` and before any tree.
        If (outcome is "ok" or outcome starts with "ok\n") and the number of warnings is greater than 0:
            Define said-warnings as a chase.
            For each warning in warnings, repeat:
                Insert "\n" into said-warnings.
                Insert cast one-line on (warning) into said-warnings.
            Done.
            Return "ok" joined to (said-warnings converted to text) joined to (the characters from 3 to the end of outcome).
        Done.
        Return outcome.
    Done.

    // `--prelude <folder>` says where the bundled books' source is; run from the repository, it is found.
    Define prelude-folder as "src/Interpreter/Prelude".
    Define files as a series of text.
    Define taking-folder as false.
    Define with-tree as false.
    For each passed in the arguments, repeat:
        If taking-folder:
            The prelude-folder becomes passed.
            The taking-folder becomes false.
        Done.
        Otherwise if passed is "--prelude":
            The taking-folder becomes true.
        Done.
        Otherwise if passed is "--tree":
            The with-tree becomes true.
        Done.
        Otherwise:
            Insert passed into files.
        Done.
    Done.
    Define prelude as cast prelude-definitions on (prelude-folder).
    // ⚠ Loud, not empty: with no prelude every pull of a bundled book would be refused, in words
    // that look like a real answer.
    If the number of prelude is 0:
        State "crash\tno bundled books were found in '{prelude-folder}' — pass --prelude <folder>".
        Exit with 1.
    Done.

    // A project's files, parsed once for the whole run — every file of a folder loads its neighbours.
    Define shared-trees as a map from text to node.
    For each name in files, repeat:
        State "file\t{name}".
        // Outside the file's region, so the trees outlive it: what is parsed in a region dies with it.
        Cast share-project-trees on (cast full-path on (name), shared-trees).
        // ⚠⚠ Each file in a region of its own, so what checking it built is gone before the next.
        // A file inside a project brings its whole folder with it, and nothing was ever handed back:
        // MEASURED 15 GB at the end of the corpus, and the compiled checker died with an access
        // violation when the test suite was running beside it.
        Pull a rabbit.
            Try to:
                // Checked where it sits: inside a project, its folder is part of its program.
                State cast verdict on (read all from the file name, prelude, with-tree, cast full-path on (name), shared-trees).
            Done.
            In case of failure:
                State "crash\t{the message of the failure}".
            Done.
            // ⚠ A fault inside the checker itself — an index past the end — would otherwise stop every
            // file after this one. Said as a `crash` line, which the parity test counts as a difference.
            In case of exception:
                State "crash\t{the message of the exception}".
                Suppress the exception.
            Done.
        Done.
    Done.
Done.
